  "analytic_review_groups": [
    "Mean normalization is the continuous image of the supremum-normalized compact set; empty potential classes and the zero-mass singleton retain their distinctions.",
    "Distributional continuity of the linear trace becomes weak measure convergence through positivity and common finite mass.",
    "Elliptic regularity and the maximum principle give injectivity up to constants; the canonical quasi-psh representative recovers pointwise equality.",
    "Gauduchon integration gives A*1=0; index zero and the constant kernel give the mean-zero Green inverse by distributional duality.",
    "Admissible positive trace measures additionally require positivity of the full current in higher dimension; on a curve this condition is automatic.",
    "Bounded Lipschitz tests separate measures and have finite uniform nets; compactness supplies completeness and uniform equivalence for different auxiliary metrics.",
    "The lower quantitative bound uses W2,p estimates with p>2n and the mean-zero adjoint equation, then L1 duality against smooth bounded functions.",
    "The upper bound retains the common trace total variation, smoothing errors O(epsilon) and adjoint bound O(epsilon^-1), including zero and large norm cases.",
    "The elliptic-curve example retains curvature amplitude 1/2, norm 1/(pi^2 k^2) and test integral 1/(8pi k); the separate t-scaling excludes improving the lower exponent.",
    "Gradient energy is the signed integration-by-parts pairing; two positive measures of mass CM give its upper bound and matrix Cauchy-Schwarz controls the gradient currents in total variation.",
    "Uniform smooth approximation with a common lower Hessian bound gives canonical gradient currents independent of the chosen approximants.",
    "The product formula cancels both nonclosed-current first-derivative terms, is positive by vanishing curvature-loss correction, and is unchanged under smooth changes of decomposition.",
    "Joint continuity uses bounded masses of the varying currents and uniform potential convergence; fixed smooth approximants separate the limits. No closure of the background or of G is assumed."
